<img src="creator-god.jpg">

<p id=Extract>Religions, the New Age movement, and any group of stoned college students 
gathered around a late-night bonfire have this in common: Whether they know it 
or not, they are pondering <i>metaphysics</i>, that is, the branch of philosophy 
investigating principles of reality transcending those of any particular 
science. In the case of religion, this investigation is centered around an 
attempt to understand God, who presumably created the principles of reality, 
which places God <i>beyond physics,</i> which is what &quot;metaphysics&quot; literally 
means (or, &quot;a physics that contains physics&quot;). The New Age movement is more 
focused on how the principles of reality can be understood and exploited to 
achieve greater happiness and fulfillment. And the college students, in addition 
to wondering whether stars are like protons and planets like electrons and, 
therefore, our Solar System could be, like, a <i>really big atom</i> and 
therefore part of, you know, something much bigger, are in search of an even 
better buzz.</p>

<p>The problem is that religions sabotage themselves by envisioning God as 
something separate and apart from His creations (and assign gender to a being 
even they claim is genderless, as well). The New Age movement, on the other hand, 
is almost entirely self-oriented (as in, &quot;we each create our own reality&quot;) and 
so misses the framework in which that reality is created. And the students have 
already left to snack at McDonald's.</p>

<p>To rectify this, in this essay I intend to present that framework. I promise 
that, if you follow this through, any further work you do in metaphysics will 
make much more sense.</p>

<img src="Flying_Squirrels.jpg">

<p>It's safe to assume that animals don't consciously worry about <i>physics,</i>
much less metaphysics. But they <i>use</i> physics constantly, as we do: An 
innate understanding of gravity and inertia is essential simply to move about. 
People tossed stones to each other for hundreds of thousands of years 
before the mathematics describing such ballistic motion and hyperbolic curves 
had been developed.</p>

<p>My point is that, one can live an entire lifetime in a gravity field without 
considering that one is <i>in</i> a gravity field. Instinctively knowing how to 
balance upright in one does not require any conscious acknowledgement of it 
whatsoever.</p>

<p>Similarly, most of us have heard the terms &quot;three dimensional&quot; and even 
&quot;fourth dimensional&quot; without having any inkling what they mean in a scientific 
sense. (Worse, New Agers and bad sci-fi movies often <i>misuse</i> the terms, 
further muddying their meaning.)</p>

<p>So let's start with the easy one, and first one understood&mdash;at least as far 
back as the 16th century. That's the third dimension, familiar to us because it 
is (a subset of) the framework in which we live our humdrum lives.</p>

<p>Length, width and height: <i>Those</i> are &quot;the&quot; three dimensions. I put the 
word &quot;the&quot; in quotes because, mathematically, <i>any</i> three points that are 
at right angles to each other make up three dimensions. But length, width and 
height are the ones we use in everyday life.</p>

<p>Your living room has a width (the distance from the wall at your left to the 
wall at your right), a length (the distance from the wall in back of you to the 
wall in front of you) and a height (the distance from floor to ceiling). Once 
you've arbitrarily selected a starting point where all three values are zero&mdash;say 
the corner to your left rear at the floor&mdash;and the maximum values (the right 
front ceiling corner, for example)&mdash;you can located <i>any spot in the room</i> 
with three numbers representing how far to the right, how far from the back, and 
how far from the floor.</p>

<p>It doesn't matter that you might not actually want to <i>do</i> that. The 
point is, that anything in the room will <i>have</i> those values, whether you 
write them down or not. What this coordinate system describes is a 
<i>3-dimensional continuum</i>, or what we commonly call a <i>space</i>. And a 
&quot;space&quot; doesn't <i>have</i> to be a living room; it can be a forest, or even all 
the room in the Universe, which is commonly called <i>outer space</i>. (And now 
you know where <i>that</i> term comes from!)</p>

<img src="3-D.jpg">

<p>Let's look again at how we derive those three coordinates. A single number 
describes one point. One point does not constitute a dimension (or, if you 
prefer, it constitutes a <i>zero-dimension</i>). <i>Any</i> direction from that 
one point can be considered ninety degrees from it. As soon as you've added a 
second point, you have a <i>line</i>&mdash;which makes up a 1-dimensional continuum, 
or &quot;line&quot;.</p>

<p>A coordinate (you can only have one) describes any position on the line. (And 
because numbers can be negative, you can even describe a position &quot;before&quot; the 
start of the line, which makes the line effectively infinite in length.)</p>

<p>Here's our line:</p>

<img src="Line.gif" alt="A line, or one-dimensional continuum.">

<p>Let's work with the line for a bit. First of all, it comprises a 
<i>1-dimensional continuum.</i> (The common name for that is &quot;line&quot;. The word 
&quot;line&quot; is the one-dimensional analog of &quot;space&quot;.)</p>

<p>Secondly, a line can be home to an infinite number of zero-dimensional 
<i>points</i>. In fact, that's what it's <i>made</i> of! Could one those points be 
self-aware? Why not? (Where do you think Sarah Palin came from?)</p>

<p>If a point <i>were</i> self-aware (in other words, a one-dimensional 
&quot;person&quot;) it would only be able to look &quot;ahead&quot; of itself and &quot;behind&quot; itself, 
and of course it's &quot;vision&quot; would be blocked by the points in front and behind. 
So its sensory life would be severely limited, not that it would know the 
difference.</p>

<p>In a 1-dimensional continuum, it is a given that nothing can exist that is 
<i>not in that line</i>. Anything that extends beyond it, is by definition not part 
of that continuum even though they may intersect&mdash;we'll consider the implications 
of that shortly.</p>

<p>When we started with a point and created a line, we were free to do so in any 
of an infinite number of directions. To go from a line to a plane is slightly 
more limited. We can only go in a direction that is 90&deg; from the line. That's 
still an infinite number of possibilities (a fraction of infinity is <i>still</i>
infinity). When we've done so&mdash;and remember, like the line, this figure also 
extends indefinitely&mdash;we have a 2-dimensional continuum, 
commonly known as a &quot;plane&quot;.</p>

<img src="Plane.gif">

<img src="Kurt_Russell.jpg" class="Left" alt="Kurt Russell wore a flat-top hairstyle in the movie &quot;Stargate&quot;.">

<p>Common examples of planes include tabletops, the Saskatchewan landscape, and Kurt 
Russell's hair in the movie <i>Stargate</i>.</p>

<p>In a 2-dimensional continuum, however, as opposed to a tabletop, nothing can 
exist that extends above or below the plane. Anything that does, is by 
definition, part of a greater continuum although the plane and this hypothetical 
object may intersect.</p>

<p>A plane may be home to an infinite number of lines, or of points. In fact, 
any number of shapes can exist there, as long as they are flat.</p>

<p>In the 1884 book <i><a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flatland">Flatland</a></i>
by Edwin Abbott Abbott, the author describes a biology and sociology for 
beings who live in such a planar universe.</p>

<blockquote>
	<p>In the book, men are portrayed as polygons whose social class is directly 
	proportional to the number of sides they have; therefore, triangles, having 
	only three sides, are at the bottom of the social ladder and are considered 
	generally unintelligent, while the Priests are composed of multi-sided 
	polygons whose shapes approximate a circle, which is considered to be the 
	&quot;perfect&quot; shape.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>Buildings are, to us, simple rectangles with cutouts for &quot;doors&quot; (because no 
Flatlander can pass through a solid line). Furniture is similarly composed of 
lines against which Flatlanders can rest or place other objects.</p>

<img src="Flatland.png">

<p>It's important to remember that Flatlanders cannot look &quot;up&quot; or &quot;down&quot; or 
even imagine what those words might mean. Left-right and fore-aft are the only 
primary directions they can perceive. They are simply not equipped for anything else.</p>

<p>&mdash;Which brings us to the next step up, the 3-dimensional continuum known as 
Space. (The capital 'S' is because we live here.) To define it, pick any four 
points on a plane and extend them together at 90&deg; from the plane to create a 
cube. As the plane can contain any number of flat objects, the space can contain 
any number of 3-dimensional objects. (And, in the case of our Space, does!)</p>

<img src="Space.gif" class="Right">

<p>Now, so far, all this has been rather intuitive&mdash;an exercise designed to 
provide you with a vocabulary for speaking about continua and dimensions. It's 
easy because we have direct experience with points, lines, planes and spaces. 
The first real challenge comes with the concept of <i>four</i> dimensions, 
because, just as the Flatlanders cannot point <i>up</i> or <i>down</i>, we 
cannot point in the direction of a fourth dimension&mdash;indeed, we don't even have 
words for those directions. Yet, just as up and down exist whether the 
Flatlanders are aware of it or not, so do those other directions.</p>

<p>Something I haven't emphasized yet is that any continuum must be <i>contained 
in a continuum of at least one greater dimension</i>. That is, a <i>line</i> 
(1-D continuum) implies a 2-D container in which it, and other lines like it, 
may reside. A <i>square</i> (2-D continuum) implies a 3-D container. And a <i>
space</i> (3-D continuum) implies a 4-D container.

<p>What might that &quot;extra&quot; dimension look like? One possibility is 
<i>time</i>. As Einstein brilliantly intuited, and then proved, <i>time</i> is <i>a</i> 4th 
dimension that can be treated mathematically the same as length, 
width and height. Obviously, we don't <i>perceive</i> time in the same way&hellip;but 
who's to say, if we were rotated about a 4th dimensional axis, that time wouldn't seem like 
distance to us and width like duration? Indeed, &quot;before&quot; and &quot;after&quot; 
may well <i>be</i> the means by which we point into this otherwise 
inaccessible dimension.</p>


